{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel shall observe, etc.</b> One needs to know why He added yet another [expression of] observance. Further, one needs to know the intent of His saying <b>to make the Sabbath</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted what they interpreted." "Or perhaps it is because He came to command that they should observe, lest they come to a stumbling block of error regarding the day, in order to make the day of the Sabbath truly. And that is His saying <b>and the children of Israel shall observe the Sabbath</b> in order <b>to make the Sabbath</b> — the known one — so that one not come to error. For even if one sanctifies the seventh day as mentioned nearby, that is not [truly] the day of the Sabbath according to the truth." "Or it may allude, according to what we explained in the preceding verse, that HaShem commands one for whom knowledge of the day has been lost to count six days and observe the seventh — for this reason He comes to give a rationale and says <b>and the children of Israel shall observe the Sabbath</b>, which is the day one sanctifies by its count, and the reason is <b>to make the Sabbath</b> — the true one — so that the commandment of the sanctified day not lapse from memory. And as for the reason He called the seventh day by the name Sabbath — do not worry about erring from the fact that the verse concludes by saying ״to make the Sabbath״ — this points with a finger, for the first Sabbath is the Sabbath that one makes by one's reckoning." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of the verse (Genesis 37:11) ״and his father kept the matter״ — meaning, he waits and looks forward to when it will come. And the intention in this is to command that the Sabbath not be a burdensome thing on account of the restraint from desired labor and the pursuit of pleasure, but rather one must rejoice in it with complete willingness and desire for the matter, and one should always be waiting and looking forward to when it will come. And His saying <b>to make the Sabbath</b> is to negate the intention of desiring the Sabbath commandment for bodily rest and for pleasure that one will enjoy, but rather for the sake of fulfilling the Sabbath commandment and not for the purpose of felt sensations." "<b>Further,</b> it intends along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Yoma 81a) that one must add from the profane onto the holy, and not refrain from labor once the day of the Sabbath has arrived and shines forth, but rather one must prepare oneself in the sanctity of Sabbath and go forth like a bridegroom to meet the bride, and sit and watch until the Sabbath arrives to make it — meaning, to perform the acts required in it. Further, it intends in His saying <b>to make the Sabbath</b> that the time one adds from the profane onto the holy, HaShem agrees to call it Sabbath, and it turns out that this person is literally making a Sabbath — for the hours of the sixth day and also the hours of the first day, which are profane, the children of Israel make them Sabbath." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to the statement of the Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 11:8): Sabbath said before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'To all of them You gave a mate, but to me You did not give a mate!' He said to her: 'Behold Israel' etc. until here. Now, since the Sabbath lacks one element of completeness, and its repair is through Israel's keeping it, this is the verse's saying <b>and the children of Israel shall keep the Sabbath</b>. What is the reason — <b>to make the Sabbath</b>: the language of repair, for through this the act of Sabbath is repaired and it is found to have a mate." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that one must keep the Sabbath so that no profane matter be in it, whether from oneself or from another. And a person should not say: 'It is enough for me that I guard myself from desecrating it, and if it is desecrated by another, what of it?' Rather, one must guard it lest it be desecrated, just as one guards one's garden and orchard. And included within this parameter is that one should strive to guard lest an unwitting act come to hand through which the Sabbath would be desecrated, along the lines of their statement of blessed memory (Shabbat 11a) 'one shall not read by lamplight,' and all the fences and guardings lest the Sabbath be desecrated even in a situation where one is technically exempt for them." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to say that this keeping is unlike all other keepings of commandments, for this keeping is reckoned in the category of doing, and it is what the verse says: <b>and they shall keep</b> etc. <b>to make</b> — see, it is right before you that it calls keeping 'doing.' Or perhaps the reason is according to their words of blessed memory who said (Kiddushin 39b): if a person sat and did not commit a transgression, he is given reward as one who performs a commandment — and they established it in the Gemara as referring to a case where a transgression came to hand and he did not transgress, until here. And so too on the holy day there is no case of a transgression coming to hand regarding labors such as this, for at all times numerous labors present themselves before him, from the forty primary labors and their derivatives, in the details of construction and the details of cooking and the details of buying and selling that a person is accustomed to all day long, presenting themselves without number, and he guards himself from them. Therefore it is reckoned for them as one who performs a commandment." "<b>Further</b>, it intends: since He preceded and commanded regarding the keeping of the Sabbath, reason would come to say that anyone who keeps the Sabbath — behold, he receives the reward of the Sabbath. Therefore HaShem commanded and said <b>and the children of Israel shall keep</b>, meaning specifically the children of Israel are the ones who are included in keeping the Sabbath, and no other nation or tongue. And therefore it did not say 'and you shall keep' (ושמרתם), for then it would be heard as coming to command the keeping, whereas it comes to exclude all but the children of Israel, that they shall not keep the Sabbath. And this is their statement of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 58b): a gentile who observes the Sabbath is liable to death, for the verse restricts them, and this warning is their death." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this line: <b>and the children of Israel shall keep the Sabbath</b>, meaning — when they keep even one Sabbath, and not only if they keep it, but even if they resolve in their minds to perform the commandment of the Sabbath, and it is the verse's saying <b>to make the Sabbath</b>: Scripture credits them as though they made it for all their generations. And accordingly, an Israelite who kept even one Sabbath and died, they do not deduct from the reward that accrues to him as if he had kept it for all generations (Mekhilta)." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what we prefaced regarding the verse ״אַךְ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתַי״ — that HaShem commanded to desecrate the Sabbath for the sake of pikuach nefesh (saving a life). Here the verse explains that the Sabbath is not diminished in its observance except in the scale of the children of Israel; but in the scale of others, to save the life of a non-Jew, even if he is a ger toshav who observes the seven commandments and is in mortal danger — an Israelite may not desecrate the Sabbath for his sake (Rambam, Laws of Sabbath 2:20). And this is what it says <b>and the children of Israel shall keep</b> — that none of them die; <b>et the Sabbath</b> — meaning, as a warning concerning the Sabbath, that they not desecrate it. Or in this manner: Sabbath and Israel are before you — this one to be desecrated and this one to die — you shall preserve Israel and the Sabbath shall be desecrated, but not for the nations. And the reason <b>la'asot et the Sabbath</b> — meaning, all of it, what reason do I give you to desecrate the Sabbath on his behalf? It is <b>la'asot et the Sabbath for their generations</b> — desecrate one Sabbath on his behalf so that he may observe many Sabbaths; but a non-Jew who is not a child of Sabbath — no." "<b>Or</b> it may intend in this manner: when did I tell you to save an Israelite even at the equivalent value of Sabbath honor? Specifically <b>la'asot</b> — meaning, in the case of a person who falls under the category of one who will stand to act; but someone who will surely not arise and will not reach the Sabbath to observe it, even though these remedies will help for hours or for days — one does not desecrate the Sabbath on his behalf." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 31a) that the World to Come is called עוֹלָם שֶׁכֻּלּוֹ שַׁבָּת — a world that is entirely Sabbath. And they further said (Exodus Rabbah 25:12) that Sabbath is weighed against the entire Torah, all of it. Therefore it says <b>and the children of Israel shall keep et the Sabbath</b>, and the reason <b>la'asot lahem et the Sabbath</b> — meaning, the world called by the name Sabbath, which they shall exist in for generation after generation. And this is what it says <b>ledorotam</b> — to exclude this world, in which a person does not endure for generations. <b>Berit olam</b> — meaning, a covenant cut for the observer of Sabbath, that he may inherit the supernal world." "<b>Further,</b> it is explained according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Beitzah 16a), who said that on the day of Sabbath an additional soul comes to Israel, as alluded to in His saying <b>shabat vayinnafash</b>, and it is a hidden sod that HaShem did not reveal to the nations, as they of blessed memory said (ibid.): neshamah yeteirah — I did not make known to them. And I awakened in the hidden inner meaning of the matter, in what the Gemara raised as a difficulty concerning the making known of Sabbath to the nations: if He did not make it known to them, they would not be punished for it. And they said: Sabbath I made known to them; neshamah yeteirah I did not make known to them — end. And it is difficult: why do they not also come with the argument and say, if He had made known to them the neshamah yeteirah, they would have accepted Sabbath and not been punished for it? And it appears that for this our Sages of blessed memory were cunning and said (Shabbat 10b): ״מַתָּנָה טוֹבָה״ — a good gift, etc. — meaning, that this matter is nothing but in the category of a gift that HaShem was pleased to give to His people, and it is not reward about which the nations could come with an argument as mentioned, for it is not the way to say, why did you not give us a gift as well. Further, you will find that Israel had already accepted upon themselves the observance of Sabbath before the giving of the neshamah yeteirah, and from now on all the nations will be punished for Sabbath, and our eyes see." "And the nature of this additional soul — our Sages hinted at its place in saying ״מַתָּנָה טוֹבָה בְּבֵית גְּנָזַי״ — a good gift in My treasure-house. And the place called ginzei ha-melekh, the King's treasuries, is known: it is a bhinah (spiritual level) of the supernal world, and this soul's quarry is there. And therefore it is called Sabbath, as they said ״וְשַׁבָּת שְׁמָהּ״ — and Sabbath is its name — like the name of the world from which it comes, which is called עוֹלָם שֶׁכֻּלּוֹ שַׁבָּת — a world that is entirely Sabbath (Rosh HaShanah 31a). And in that world there is no sorrow, and upon it comes none, according to their words, except delight and joy. And therefore HaShem commanded to distance on the day of Sabbath all those bhinot of the profane — labor and affliction — for through any one of them the soul would be repelled from dwelling within a person. And therefore you will find that the essential prohibition regarding the warning against labors of Sabbath, HaShem explained that they are in thought, as they said (Beitzah 13a): ״מְלֶאכֶת מַחֲשֶׁבֶת אָסְרָה תּוֹרָה״ — the Torah prohibited melakhet machashevet (deliberate labor). And even profane speech is forbidden (Shabbat 113a), as it is written (Isaiah 58:13): ״מִמְּצוֹא חֶפְצְךָ וְדַבֵּר דָּבָר״. And all this is because of the addition of the supernal soul, to act before it according to the order of the world from which it comes. And this is what HaShem commanded in His saying <b>and the children of Israel shall keep et the Sabbath</b> — meaning, the soul called Sabbath; and the ultimate purpose of this guarding is the attainment of the world whose name is Sabbath. And this is His saying <b>la'asot et the Sabbath.</b> And know that it is absolutely impossible for a person to ascend to the supernal world except through the mediation of attaining a branch of it while he is in this world; and therefore, if Israel do not attain this bhinah of soul, they have no capacity to stand at the level of the supernal. And this is His saying <b>la'asot et the Sabbath</b> — meaning, through its mediation you shall make for yourselves the Sabbath, so that you can attain, stand there, and let your souls delight in the supernal treasuries. And His saying <b>ledorotam</b> — for there all the generations of the children of Israel will be gathered, which is the bhinah of the World to Come." "<b>Further</b>, it intends the sense of dwelling, and therefore it is written defectively without a vav, and this is what we said — that it alludes to the place of dwelling of the souls, which is the World to Come, for which HaShem cut a covenant to give us, and this is His saying <b>an everlasting covenant.</b> Or perhaps in His saying ברית עולם He alludes to the 613 commandments, for the numerical value of ברית is 612, and all of them depend on Shabbat — behold, 613; and therefore He juxtaposed the word ״ברית״ to ״עולם״ to say that through the observance of Shabbat one fulfills all 613 commandments, for it is equivalent to all of them, and behold, it is עולם (its world)." "And His saying <b>between Me and between the children of Israel</b> — meaning, no stranger is with us in that world. Or perhaps even the angels will not sit in our company in this world, for it is above the world of the angels. Along these lines is His saying (Numbers 23:23) ״כָּעֵת יֵאָמֵר לְיַעֲקֹב וּלְיִשְׂרָאֵל מַה פָּעַל אֵל״, and they said of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:20) that the angels will ask Israel, who are drawn near to El, מה פעל אל." "Further, it intends <b>between Me and between the children of Israel</b> — that the secret of the matters will be, and no person in the world will know them; and this is their saying of blessed memory (Beitzah 16a) who said: the additional soul — we do not inform them, for HaShem has no desire that the nations know the secrets of El Hai, and this is His saying (Psalms 147:19–20) ״חֻקָּיו וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו לְיִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא עָשָׂה כֵן לְכָל גּוֹי״, and they said of blessed memory (Chagigah 13a) etc. And His saying <b>it is a sign forever</b> — meaning, since all this will be a secret for Israel and they will not reveal the matter, if so, should one come to ask about the commandment of the day: ״What makes this day different from all other days? What sign is made in it to effect all the changes in it?״ — what would our answer to him be? Therefore He said ״<b>it is a sign forever</b>״ — meaning, that it will be revealed to all who come into the world, because six days etc., <b>and on the seventh day He rested</b> etc. This reason will be revealed to those outside us, but with the children of Israel their reason is hidden with them, sealed in the treasuries of life."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:08.693221441Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}